\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri-432\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin-432\lin0\itap0 {\f1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid0\langnp1033 
\par }\pard\plain \s63\qj \li0\ri-432\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin-432\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
The Geometry Builder is a 4nec2 helper program to create more or less complex geometry structures such as surface/ground planes, partially open or closed box- or cylindrical-shaped structures,  parabolas and/or helices.
\par 
\par The output of the Geometry Builder is a Nec-type input-file (placed in the folder where you last opened a 4nec2 input-file). To create a structure, select the appropriate structure, enter the required dimensions and angles and click the \lquote Create
\rquote  button. Use the check boxes to select if one or the other side should left open. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f28\fs20\lang1043\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1043\langfenp1033 {\f1\lang2057\langfe1033\langnp2057 
Unfortunately however, I managed to mesh up the naming conventions for wires and sections between the dimensions for the different structures. A surface is modelled using a number of wires. A wire may be divided in a number of sections
 where other (perpendicular) wires are crossing.
\par 
\par For each dimension of the structure to create, the number of wires/section is specified in the first entry box. The number of segments for each wire/section is specified in the second entry box. 
\par }\pard\plain \s30\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f28\fs20\lang1043\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1043\langfenp1033 {\f1\lang2057\langfe1033\langnp2057 
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f28\fs20\lang1043\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1043\langfenp1033 {\b\f1\lang2057\langfe1033\langnp2057 Note:}{\f1\lang2057\langfe1033\langnp2057  If 
a zero (0) is specified as the number of segments, the wire(s) is/are omitted from the model.
\par 
\par If \lquote auto-segmentation\rquote  is set, any specified number of segments unequal zero (> 0) is overruled by the calculated number of segments. All this depending on frequency and segmentation density.
\par 
\par When modelling a surface using a wire-grid it\rquote s recommended to use a number of 10 wires/section for each wavelength.  Where current-levels are expected to be high or change rapidly from one wire-grid element to the next a so
mewhat higher count could be used. 
\par 
\par Ideally, the surface area of the wire-grid should approximate the area of the plane being modelled. This often leads to the use of fairly fat wires, since the grid wire radius should equal the grid spacing dived by 2*Pi.
\par 
\par When \lquote use equal-area rule\rquote  is set, the geometry builder automatically calculates the required wire-radius (except for the helix) using above recommendation. If not set, you will have to enter the radius manually.
\par 
\par After having created a structure you can use the 4nec2 (geometry) editor to manually remove any unwanted segments or wires. Sometimes they are generated due to the finite accuracy of the geometry-builder.
\par \line To create more complex models you can manually merge two or more geometry-builder output-files. When doing so, use 4nec2 to check if wire-junctions are connected as expected and run geometry-check to see if wires are not overlaid or crossing.
\par 
\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri-432\nowidctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin-432\lin0\itap0 {\f1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid0\langnp1033 
\par 
